WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.079 --> 00:00:06.200
When thinking about the life of Bruce Wayne,
many can agree that his most famous life event

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:08.250
is his most tragic.

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:12.530
Most iterations of the Batman story, at some
point, have depicted the event that sealed

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:15.290
his fate: the murder of his parents.

00:00:15.290 --> 00:00:19.600
It's a moment that changes the course of his
life forever, driving him to create a symbol

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:21.710
that the criminals of Gotham will fear.

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:26.720
Matt Reeves' "The Batman," unlike so many
previous cinematic incarnations of the character,

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:29.119
does not tread on this familiar territory.

00:00:29.119 --> 00:00:34.089
When "The Batman" picks up, it is two years
after Bruce has started wearing the cowl.

00:00:34.089 --> 00:00:38.710
He feels the trauma of his past deeply, but
it is represented more in what is not said,

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:39.710
than what is.

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:42.479
"I don't care what happens to me."

00:00:42.479 --> 00:00:48.150
Bruce has become reclusive, hiding away in
Wayne Tower with Alfred, played by Andy Serkis,

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:52.220
only venturing out around people when it will
help him solve a mystery.

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:56.119
There are many aspects about this version
of Batman that are heartbreaking.

00:00:56.119 --> 00:01:01.010
Bruce's self-imposed isolation, and his inability
to connect to other people in a meaningful

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:02.619
way, are just a couple.

00:01:02.619 --> 00:01:07.250
And in the midst of all the murder and mayhem
the film depicts, it is one scene in particular

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:11.600
that shows just how sad the life of Gotham's
Dark Knight truly is.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:15.940
Throughout the film, Bruce has made it abundantly
clear how alone he is.

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:19.140
He goes out at night and fights as many criminals
as he can.

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:23.310
When he comes home, the only person who tries
to forge a connection with him is Alfred,

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:24.630
but it's a one-sided effort.

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:28.590
"If this continues, it won't be long before
you've nothing left."

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:33.350
Alfred is concerned about Bruce's nighttime
activities and why a serial killer is leaving

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:34.370
him messages.

00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:39.170
But for all of Alfred's attempts to take care
of Bruce, the young Wayne pushes him away.

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:43.050
He tells Alfred that he isn't his father and
doesn't accept his guidance.

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:47.100
This dynamic reaches its devastating peak
when the Riddler sends a package to Wayne

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:48.100
Tower.

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:53.270
While it's meant for Bruce, Alfred opens it,
just as a bomb inside is detonated.

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:55.910
Alfred survives the attack, but he is terribly
wounded.

00:01:55.910 --> 00:02:00.799
When he regains consciousness in the hospital,
he and Bruce have a loaded conversation, full

00:02:00.799 --> 00:02:03.250
of pain and unresolved trauma.

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:07.690
Both Alfred and Bruce have burdens they need
to unload upon one another, and when they

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:10.229
do, it finally brings them together.

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:14.120
When Alfred first wakes up from his injuries,
Bruce is there by his side.

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:19.290
But rather than express sympathy, the Dark
Knight's first response is to accuse his longtime

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:22.220
butler of keeping family secrets from him.

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:26.650
Bruce lashes out, upset that he never knew
his father had a business relationship with

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:28.910
known mobster Carmine Falcone.

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:33.430
However, once the two resolve this issue,
Bruce admits to being afraid for the first

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:35.599
time since the death of his parents.

00:02:35.599 --> 00:02:39.230
To cope with the loss, he became a symbol
devoid of any fear.

00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:44.880
By becoming the personification of vengeance,
he ensured he would never feel powerless again.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:49.400
But Bruce acknowledges that when Alfred's
life was in danger, being Batman did not help.

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:53.630
He admits that Alfred is one of the only people
that means something to him anymore, and that

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:56.910
losing him would be just as bad as losing
his parents.

00:02:56.910 --> 00:02:59.989
Bruce wasn't the only one who has been holding
onto past pain.

00:02:59.989 --> 00:03:03.239
Alfred holds immense guilt over the deaths
of the Waynes.

00:03:03.239 --> 00:03:07.790
As he tells his young charge, Bruce was "just
a boy" when they died, and Alfred thinks he

00:03:07.790 --> 00:03:10.190
should have done better to protect the Waynes.

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:14.130
Not only that, but he never felt equipped
to take care of a young Bruce or raise him

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:17.490
properly, after being thrust into a parental
role.

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:21.380
Alfred remarks that Bruce needed a father,
and he never felt that he was good enough

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:22.680
to fill that role.

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:28.530
He blames himself for Bruce's emotional withdrawal,
repression, and rejection of the Wayne legacy.

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:33.349
That said, it is clear that Alfred holds no
ill will towards Bruce, despite how cold their

00:03:33.349 --> 00:03:35.890
relationship appears earlier in the film.

00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:40.319
This scene marks the first time both men have
truly opened up to one another, and it allows

00:03:40.319 --> 00:03:43.550
them to finally begin healing their relationship.

